Article 16

Forms of services for the protection of women and women victims of violence include:
a. complaint, consultation and counseling services;
b. assistance services;
c. health services;
d. social rehabilitation services;
e. legal services and/or legal aid; and
f. repatriation and social reintegration services.

Article 18

1. Assistance services as referred to in Article 16 letter b include:
a. assisting victims during the examination and health recovery process;
b. assisting victims during the medical examination process for legal purposes;
c. assisting victims during the examination process at the police, prosecutor's office and court;
d. monitoring the interests and rights of victims in the examination process at the police, prosecutor's office and court;
e. maintaining the privacy and confidentiality of victims from all interested parties, including coverage by mass media;
f. coordinating with other assistants; and
g. providing rehabilitation.
2. Assistance Services as referred to in paragraph (1) constitute continuous handling services up to the stage of activities and actions carried out by assistants from individuals or representatives of institutions that have expertise in assisting victims to conduct counseling, therapy, and advocacy for the strengthening and recovery of victims of violence.

Article 19

Health services as referred to in Article 16 letter c shall be implemented by the Regional Apparatus responsible for health affairs in the form of:
a. medical assistance to victims;
b. treatment and recovery of physical injuries aimed at restoring the physical condition of victims carried out by medical and paramedical personnel;
c. implementation of visum et repertum for investigation purposes;
d. provision of services in accordance with SOPs to women victims of violence; and
e. health service referrals in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 23

1. Empowerment of women victims of violence shall be carried out in an integrated manner in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
2. Empowerment of women victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be directed to obtain opportunities and rights as human beings to be able to play a role and participate in activities:
a. health;
b. education;
c. economy;
d. law;
e. employment;
f. politics and government.

Article 26

The organization of Women's Empowerment in the economic field as referred to in Article 23 paragraph (2) letter c shall be implemented by the Regional Apparatus responsible for micro, small and medium enterprises and other related agencies in the form of:
a. provision of skills and job training;
b. facilitation of the formation and strengthening of productive economic business groups;
c. facilitation of the development of marketing networks.

CHAPTER V

OBLIGATIONS AND RESPONSIBILITIES

Article 30

protection of women victims of violence The Regional Government is obliged to:
a. ensure the implementation of the fulfillment of women's rights and the provision of guarantees of security from acts of violence, and efforts to exploit women in the region;
b. establish policies, programs, activities for the protection of women and guarantees for handling victims to eliminate all forms of discrimination and acts of violence against women in the region;
c. provide guidance and supervision in efforts to protect women carried out by the Regional Apparatus handling the Protection and Empowerment of Women;
d. strive for the establishment of women's protection institutions;
e. establish policies, programs and activities for the protection of women that have been established; and
f. conduct socialization of laws and regulations relating to the protection of women victims of violence and discrimination;

Article 31

The Regional Government is responsible for:
a. carrying out activities to ensure the implementation of the fulfillment of women's rights;
b. providing facilities and infrastructure for the protection of women;
c. applying minimum service standards in women's protection services and standard operating procedures for repatriation and social reintegration that apply;
d. providing implementing officers and functional officers at the UPTD PPA;
e. involving the community in the organization of women's protection.
f. providing adequate budget for women's protection programs according to regional capacity; and
g. conducting guidance and supervision over the organization of women's protection in the Region.

CHAPTER X

ADMINISTRATIVE SANCTIONS

Article 40

1. Any person or institution or entity that intentionally fails to provide protection to women resulting in acts of violence against women, allows violence to occur, and/or fails to report to the relevant agency may be subject to administrative sanctions.
2. Business Entities or non-governmental organizations shall provide Protection to Women and against Violence in their work environment.
3. Business Entities or non-governmental organizations shall report acts of violence in their work environment to the relevant agency.
4. Violations as referred to in paragraph (1), paragraph (2) and paragraph (3) may be subject to administrative sanctions in the form of:
a. verbal warning;
b. written warning;
c. temporary suspension from activities;
d. termination of cooperation;
e. revocation of registration certificate;
f. revocation of operational license;
g. sealing;
h. withdrawal of facilities; and/or
i. administrative fine of at least Rp.10,000,000,-(Ten Million Rupiah) and at most Rp. 50,000,000,-(Fifty Million Rupiah)
5. Provisions regarding the procedures for imposing administrative sanctions as referred to in paragraph (4) shall be regulated in a Regent Regulation.
